<li>Quizzes 1–4 come from Classera (Quizzes 1–3 for 7-1 from the updated report). A student who did not take Quiz 4 is shown as “لم تختبر” (did not take the test), and her quiz average uses the quizzes she took.</li>
<li>Participation /10 = PA + E + L + B + PR, 2 marks each, averaged over the weeks with complete data.</li>
<li>From week 6, PA (attendance): no absence 2, one 1.5, two 1, three or more 0.5. Absences are days in in-person weeks (paper record) and missed periods in online weeks (Classera). Week 7: Classera attendance report, no absences for any student.</li>
<li>E (engagement) follows the teacher's observation of each student: excellent 2, good 1.5, average or low 1. Every student takes part in the Tuesday lab activity and Kahoot games, so the minimum is 1, except one student who does not take part at all (0.5). Week 12 (two days in-person, three online) is weighted 2:3.</li>
<li>L (listening) is 2 for students with good or excellent participation and 1.5 for others, lower for students noted as talking a lot in class. B (behaviour) is 2 unless the teacher noted disruptive behaviour in class, judged in in-person weeks only. Two or more absences in a week lower E and L by 0.5.</li>
<li>PR (preparation): every student brings her book, so PR is 2 when that week's homework was submitted and 1.5 when it was not; in weeks with no homework (weeks 12–13) PR is 2.</li>
<li>Homework per week (Classera, digital), following the school rubric: on time and fully correct 7.5, on time with mistakes 6, 1–3 days late 4.5, 4+ days late or a score of 0 3, not submitted 1.5. Homework /15 = 2 × the weekly average (no written-homework marks recorded).</li>
<li>STEM project, Science part (7-1): the practical part (/5) is credited in full to every student because the term is online. 7-2 and 7-3: Science research paper only (/5). A group's research paper gets its mark (/5) once the paper is complete and correct.</li>
<li>Follow-up flag: quiz average below 60%, fewer than half the homework submitted, the latest quiz at least 3 marks below Quiz 1, or 12 or more absences.</li>
<li>Comparison statistics: mean, median, SD (population standard deviation), lowest, highest, range and middle half (first to third quartile). Correlation r is Pearson's, from −1 to 1.</li>
